    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Managed aquifer recharge (MAR), the intentional act of recharging water into an aquifer for later recovery, is becoming an increasingly important tool for balancing water supply and demand. Three objectives commonly defined for MAR are to 1) maximize the amount of water infiltrated, 2) maximize the amount of water recovered, and 3) maximize the improvement in quality of recovered water. In order to achieve these objectives, information is required about the subsurface properties at the MAR site. This research focuses on the use of geophysical techniques - Electrical Resistivity Tomography [ERT] and Distributed Temperature Sensing [DTS], which were used to study the Prairie MAR project in Colorado. Data gathered from these techniques were combined with existing well log and tracer-test data to improve our understanding of the MAR site.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
